

[38;5;231m⏺[39m I explored the repo before asking anything. Here's what's there:

  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — plain browser script (loaded via [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m), holds [38;5;153mconst API_ENDPOINT = [39m
    [38;5;153m"https://api.example.com/login"[39m at line 2. This is the only API endpoint config in the repo.
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a separate CommonJS Node entry point, unrelated to the browser app.
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — no dependencies, no build step, no test runner.

  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m Not because the edit is big — moving one constant is two lines — but because the
  request names a new module and a way to switch environments, and this repo has neither. There's no existing config
  flow to change, and the browser side has no module system at all today, so how the settings module gets loaded is a
  real design decision rather than a mechanical move. I'll work through questions, then a design, then a spec. If that
  feels heavier than you want, say so and I'll drop to a short in-chat design.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything else: [1mhow should the environment get selected at runtime?[0m

  The fork: the settings module has to know [3mwhich[0m environment it's in, and this app has no build step and no bundler, so
  there's nowhere for a [38;5;153mNODE_ENV[39m-style value to come from unless we add one.

  - [1mDetect from [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[0m — zero new tooling; [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m maps to dev, your staging host to staging,
    everything else to prod. Costs you a hostname table inside the settings module, and it can't distinguish two
    environments served from the same host. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mA separate, gitignored [38;5;153mconfig.local.js[39m loaded by an earlier [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag[0m — explicit and overridable per machine, no
    build step. Costs one more script tag and a file that has to exist (or be tolerated as missing) in every deployment.
    Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd a build step (bundler) that injects the environment at build time[0m — the conventional answer for real
    deployments, and it makes [38;5;153mapp.js[39m a real module. Costs you a toolchain and a build in a repo that currently has zero
    dependencies. Expensive to undo.

  My recommendation is hostname detection. It matches the repo's no-dependency, no-build character, needs nothing at
  deploy time, and if you later add a bundler the settings module's public interface ([38;5;153msettings.apiEndpoint[39m) doesn't
  change — only its internals do.

